1
00:00:43,002 --> 00:00:45,337
CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

2
00:00:46,422 --> 00:00:47,339
That man…

3
00:00:55,097 --> 00:00:59,560
CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

4
00:01:01,145 --> 00:01:02,855
…is indeed my father, Kang San.

5
00:01:04,273 --> 00:01:07,026
That man is your father?

6
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:09,028
Then…

7
00:01:11,030 --> 00:01:12,907
Your Korean name.

8
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:15,659
What is it, Chris?

9
00:01:27,838 --> 00:01:28,672
Chris.

10
00:01:29,173 --> 00:01:31,592
Your Korean name.

11
00:01:32,468 --> 00:01:33,552
What is it?

12
00:02:33,904 --> 00:02:34,780
My name is…

13
00:02:38,284 --> 00:02:39,326
Kang Han.

14
00:02:53,674 --> 00:02:54,550
What is all this?

15
00:02:57,052 --> 00:02:59,430
Who told you to go off script?

16
00:02:59,513 --> 00:03:01,849
Where did you get all those photos?

17
00:03:03,893 --> 00:03:04,935
Or…

18
00:03:09,023 --> 00:03:10,357
CAPTAIN COOK

19
00:03:10,941 --> 00:03:12,610
Was there a whole other script?

20
00:03:13,944 --> 00:03:15,613
Well, the thing is…

21
00:03:16,405 --> 00:03:18,490
Tell me, what comes next?

22
00:03:18,574 --> 00:03:20,743
Are there any more surprises?

23
00:03:21,410 --> 00:03:23,579
Where should I cut to?

24
00:03:24,079 --> 00:03:25,623
We've been stalling for too long.

25
00:03:25,706 --> 00:03:28,250
Oh, let's get a full shot of the stage.

26
00:03:31,462 --> 00:03:33,297
Captain Cook. Next question, please.

27
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,424
When Chris K. was young…

28
00:03:39,553 --> 00:03:40,387
Move.

29
00:03:41,263 --> 00:03:42,181
What?

30
00:03:42,264 --> 00:03:43,974
I said move.

31
00:03:46,185 --> 00:03:47,061
What the…

32
00:03:47,144 --> 00:03:49,063
Get out of my seat right now!

33
00:04:25,474 --> 00:04:29,395
The viewers are demanding
we reveal the next mission already.

34
00:04:29,478 --> 00:04:31,605
Our website and chat room are blowing up.

35
00:04:32,356 --> 00:04:37,653
Interacting with viewers in real time
and acting on their feedback

36
00:04:37,736 --> 00:04:41,031
make live shows so much fun.

37
00:04:42,116 --> 00:04:43,200
Okay then.

38
00:04:43,283 --> 00:04:47,329
We want to give the chefs
as much time as possible to cook,

39
00:04:47,830 --> 00:04:51,208
so we'll stop guessing who's who
from their childhood photos.

40
00:04:51,792 --> 00:04:52,793
I will now announce

41
00:04:53,377 --> 00:04:55,337
the theme of this mission.

42
00:04:57,131 --> 00:04:57,965
Here it is!

43
00:05:30,581 --> 00:05:32,291
CHEONGJU

44
00:05:48,057 --> 00:05:48,891
Enter stage.

45
00:05:49,475 --> 00:05:50,309
Cue.

46
00:06:15,334 --> 00:06:17,336
Chefs, please head to the kitchens.

47
00:06:48,408 --> 00:06:50,536
JEHYANGJU

48
00:07:52,764 --> 00:07:56,685
When it comes to this wine,
fewer ingredients mean more.

49
00:08:00,314 --> 00:08:01,440
It is matured for 100 days.

50
00:08:02,107 --> 00:08:05,569
You must bear the long wait
to enjoy the great gift.

51
00:08:12,284 --> 00:08:13,911
Many traditional recipes were lost

52
00:08:13,994 --> 00:08:17,831
due to the ban on home-brewing
during the Japanese colonial period.

53
00:08:20,834 --> 00:08:23,045
Despite being made
with homegrown rice, our water,

54
00:08:23,128 --> 00:08:25,464
and nuruk,
the traditional fermentation starter…

55
00:08:27,507 --> 00:08:32,512
cheongju, the traditional Korean liquor,
could not be called by its own name.

56
00:09:30,529 --> 00:09:32,281
CHEONGJU

57
00:09:35,701 --> 00:09:38,787
Jehyangju was made here in Haeon
with local ingredients.

58
00:09:39,705 --> 00:09:44,084
But soon, it will vanish into history
and remain only in our memories.

59
00:09:45,294 --> 00:09:48,130
With it, please help restore
the original name of our traditional wine,

60
00:09:48,839 --> 00:09:50,173
cheongju.

61
00:10:02,853 --> 00:10:03,979
What are you up to?

62
00:10:08,817 --> 00:10:09,901
Chun-ja, you…

63
00:10:15,866 --> 00:10:18,327
You shouldn't be here right now.

64
00:10:22,706 --> 00:10:23,749
What does that mean?

65
00:10:24,708 --> 00:10:26,376
Explain so I can understand.

66
00:10:27,961 --> 00:10:29,046
Answer me!

67
00:10:33,091 --> 00:10:34,426
You saw it for yourself.

68
00:10:36,678 --> 00:10:40,599
If you don't get what's happening
after seeing your son for yourself…

69
00:10:43,268 --> 00:10:45,937
What else can I say
to help you understand?

70
00:10:50,942 --> 00:10:51,777
Right?

71
00:10:53,195 --> 00:10:54,112
Chairwoman Moon?

72
00:11:05,374 --> 00:11:06,208
Chris!

73
00:11:07,626 --> 00:11:09,628
-Chris.
-Chris. What's wrong?

74
00:11:09,711 --> 00:11:11,254
-Chris.
-Wake up, Chris.

75
00:11:11,838 --> 00:11:14,257
-Michael, go get the crew. Go!
-Yes, Chef.

76
00:11:14,341 --> 00:11:17,052
Chris. Turn him onto his back.
Chris, are you okay?

77
00:11:17,636 --> 00:11:20,055
Chris, wake up. Chris!

78
00:11:21,223 --> 00:11:22,557
What's going on?

79
00:11:23,266 --> 00:11:24,851
Chris K. passed out.

80
00:11:24,935 --> 00:11:26,019
Moving to Lab One.

81
00:11:38,073 --> 00:11:39,282
Darn it.

82
00:11:39,366 --> 00:11:40,700
Ms. Seon.

83
00:11:40,784 --> 00:11:42,035
The chat room's going crazy.

84
00:11:42,119 --> 00:11:44,037
No way. Did Chris K. just collapse?

85
00:11:44,121 --> 00:11:46,790
-What's happening? Does anyone know?
-An accident, perhaps?

86
00:11:46,873 --> 00:11:49,292
All sorts of things happen on a live show.

87
00:11:51,002 --> 00:11:52,546
Cut to the outdoor stage.

88
00:11:52,629 --> 00:11:54,339
When does the drum performance end?

89
00:11:54,423 --> 00:11:56,049
In about 30 seconds.

90
00:11:57,134 --> 00:11:58,343
Thirty seconds?

91
00:11:58,427 --> 00:12:01,388
Captain Cook, talk to the audience
after the drum performance.

92
00:12:01,888 --> 00:12:02,848
Say anything.

93
00:12:02,931 --> 00:12:05,267
We'll bring
Mr. Park Seon-woong to the stage.

94
00:12:05,350 --> 00:12:07,394
Ask him about Jehyangju.

95
00:12:08,228 --> 00:12:10,939
Come on, stall for us.
Something happened on this end.

96
00:12:11,857 --> 00:12:14,025
Don't show the kitchens
on the outdoor screen.

97
00:12:14,109 --> 00:12:14,901
Okay.

98
00:12:15,735 --> 00:12:19,656
Producer Yoon.
Get Mr. Park Seon-woong to the stage now!

99
00:12:25,245 --> 00:12:26,079
Mr. Park.

100
00:12:27,372 --> 00:12:28,790
I need to talk to you.

101
00:12:31,585 --> 00:12:33,170
How long have you known?

102
00:12:35,797 --> 00:12:37,257
When did you find out?

103
00:12:37,966 --> 00:12:40,218
I don't think that's important right now.

104
00:12:40,802 --> 00:12:41,636
What?

105
00:12:42,220 --> 00:12:44,264
Tomorrow morning… No.

106
00:12:44,764 --> 00:12:46,516
After today's live show,

107
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:50,520
reporters will follow you everywhere,
demanding an explanation.

108
00:12:51,229 --> 00:12:52,439
"You had a son?"

109
00:12:53,190 --> 00:12:55,066
"Didn't you say he died long ago?"

110
00:12:55,150 --> 00:12:56,568
"Is he really your son?"

111
00:12:56,651 --> 00:12:58,820
"What happened to your husband?"

112
00:12:58,904 --> 00:13:01,031
"Why did your son show up only now?"

113
00:13:01,114 --> 00:13:04,618
"Were the father and son
living together or apart?"

114
00:13:04,701 --> 00:13:06,411
"Why did you part ways with your husband?"

115
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:11,208
Someone will quickly
dig into your family history

116
00:13:11,291 --> 00:13:13,752
and put together a family tree
for everyone to see.

117
00:13:13,835 --> 00:13:15,003
Someone else might offer

118
00:13:15,086 --> 00:13:18,423
a very interesting view
on this competition.

119
00:13:19,174 --> 00:13:20,342
"Wait a minute."

120
00:13:21,092 --> 00:13:25,305
"Was the plan to get
her secret biological son to win?"

121
00:13:25,388 --> 00:13:28,642
"Is that why she spent a fortune
to put up this whole show

122
00:13:28,725 --> 00:13:30,435
for the supposed future of K-food?"

123
00:13:30,519 --> 00:13:32,062
"She canceled the Plate In Asia system

124
00:13:32,646 --> 00:13:35,357
and talked about going back
to the roots as a food company."

125
00:13:36,566 --> 00:13:38,068
"Oh my gosh!"

126
00:13:38,151 --> 00:13:41,321
"Was all that a bit fat lie?"

127
00:13:51,623 --> 00:13:52,749
Oh no.

128
00:13:56,711 --> 00:13:58,588
What should I do now?

129
00:14:01,424 --> 00:14:02,425
What?

130
00:14:02,509 --> 00:14:04,302
What should you do now?

131
00:14:05,470 --> 00:14:08,139
Are you asking me that?

132
00:14:09,474 --> 00:14:10,475
What…

133
00:14:36,751 --> 00:14:37,752
What shall I do for you?

134
00:14:39,045 --> 00:14:41,172
What do you want me to do?

135
00:14:41,798 --> 00:14:44,050
Shall I reveal the past?

136
00:14:46,261 --> 00:14:49,347
You sent your husband to prison
for something he didn't do

137
00:14:49,431 --> 00:14:51,975
and made him sign away
his parental rights.

138
00:14:53,643 --> 00:14:55,979
Your son escaped to find his dad,

139
00:14:56,062 --> 00:14:58,898
but you caught him,
locked him up, and abused him.

140
00:15:00,150 --> 00:15:02,861
Wouldn't that beat any soap opera?

141
00:15:02,944 --> 00:15:05,447
No, wait. If we're going that far…

142
00:15:05,530 --> 00:15:08,575
Yes. If we're telling the truth,
let's make it a grand spectacle!

143
00:15:08,658 --> 00:15:10,327
Make something of it!

144
00:15:10,410 --> 00:15:12,495
You followed your husband
to the US to harass him.

145
00:15:12,579 --> 00:15:14,831
Eventually, you killed him

146
00:15:15,707 --> 00:15:18,752
and abandoned your son too.

147
00:15:18,835 --> 00:15:19,669
Yes.

148
00:15:20,879 --> 00:15:22,380
That's it.

149
00:15:22,464 --> 00:15:25,925
That's the climax we want.
Going out with a bang!

150
00:15:26,009 --> 00:15:28,053
What a story!

151
00:15:34,142 --> 00:15:35,352
What…

152
00:15:36,478 --> 00:15:37,604
What are you talking about?

153
00:15:38,188 --> 00:15:40,357
Who killed whom? Why would I kill him?

154
00:15:40,440 --> 00:15:42,984
Why are you talking such nonsense?

155
00:15:52,786 --> 00:15:53,953
You don't remember?

156
00:15:55,038 --> 00:15:55,997
What you said to me

157
00:15:56,581 --> 00:15:57,415
that day

158
00:15:58,124 --> 00:15:59,459
when you had me on my knees.

159
00:16:14,474 --> 00:16:16,559
Sometimes, to frighten one mouse away,

160
00:16:18,687 --> 00:16:21,981
-we must burn down the whole house.
-"We must burn down the whole house."

161
00:16:31,741 --> 00:16:33,493
You said he could die.

162
00:16:37,497 --> 00:16:39,874
That's what you said.

163
00:16:45,505 --> 00:16:47,757
You said he could die

164
00:16:47,841 --> 00:16:49,467
if only you could get your son back.

165
00:16:51,010 --> 00:16:51,886
You…

166
00:16:51,970 --> 00:16:55,223
That's what you said.

167
00:16:56,558 --> 00:16:57,684
You said that!

168
00:16:58,852 --> 00:17:00,770
You said it yourself!

169
00:17:08,820 --> 00:17:10,655
Hello, sir.

170
00:17:10,739 --> 00:17:12,031
Hello, good evening.

171
00:17:12,115 --> 00:17:13,032
Hello.

172
00:17:14,534 --> 00:17:16,995
What thought came to mind first?

173
00:17:19,664 --> 00:17:22,375
Making nuruk…

174
00:17:22,459 --> 00:17:25,503
We'll show the kitchens
on the outdoor screen now.

175
00:17:26,087 --> 00:17:28,173
Not Lab Two, though.

176
00:17:28,256 --> 00:17:30,008
Show Labs One and Three only.

177
00:17:30,091 --> 00:17:30,925
Okay.

178
00:17:33,386 --> 00:17:35,388
Producer Yoon,
come to the van immediately.

179
00:17:47,942 --> 00:17:50,320
-Shall I add more mayo or gochujang?
-Let's add gochujang.

180
00:17:50,403 --> 00:17:52,071
THIS IS NOT GIMBAP

181
00:17:57,494 --> 00:17:58,745
Cut to Lab Three.

182
00:18:26,606 --> 00:18:28,775
-What the heck?
-Hey!

183
00:18:29,692 --> 00:18:30,527
Oh boy.

184
00:18:42,455 --> 00:18:44,541
We can't show Lab Two yet!

185
00:18:45,917 --> 00:18:48,127
Why won't they show our team?

186
00:18:49,087 --> 00:18:50,421
Did I miss them?

187
00:18:50,505 --> 00:18:51,506
I don't know.

188
00:18:52,632 --> 00:18:53,925
What's going on?

189
00:18:55,802 --> 00:18:56,636
I don't like this.

190
00:18:56,719 --> 00:18:59,973
Do you think
something happened off camera?

191
00:19:02,600 --> 00:19:05,353
I'm in the audience
watching the stage screen.

192
00:19:05,436 --> 00:19:06,896
Something happened in Lab Three.

193
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:10,108
Why is it just Exception in Lab Two?
I don't see Chris K.

194
00:19:10,191 --> 00:19:12,944
I saw on TV earlier that he fell over.

195
00:19:13,027 --> 00:19:16,197
Shouldn't they add some graphics
to explain the situation?

196
00:19:16,281 --> 00:19:19,033
-Come on, fill us in.
-Did something happen to Chris K.?

197
00:19:19,617 --> 00:19:21,411
Ms. Seon,
the chat room's blowing up again.

198
00:19:25,748 --> 00:19:27,333
Team Three, why aren't you ready yet?

199
00:19:28,376 --> 00:19:29,210
Ms. Seon!

200
00:19:43,808 --> 00:19:44,934
Why are you here already?

201
00:19:45,018 --> 00:19:46,936
The judge interview is a long way away.

202
00:19:47,020 --> 00:19:49,272
Don't ask.
I was told to interview anyone available.

203
00:19:49,355 --> 00:19:50,481
What? Says who?

204
00:19:50,565 --> 00:19:52,442
Ms. Seon, of course. Get out of the way.

205
00:19:55,820 --> 00:19:56,696
Hello.

206
00:20:18,885 --> 00:20:19,969
Stop looking over at me.

207
00:20:21,179 --> 00:20:23,890
If they're not back in 20 minutes,
we move on to our Plan B.

208
00:20:24,515 --> 00:20:26,351
Mix ssamjang and Jehyangju
in the right ratio

209
00:20:26,434 --> 00:20:27,518
and marinate the pork jowl.

210
00:20:28,686 --> 00:20:30,813
It must sit for at least an hour
before cooking.

211
00:20:31,397 --> 00:20:32,231
Yes, Chef.

212
00:20:43,034 --> 00:20:44,202
Chris.

213
00:20:48,957 --> 00:20:51,668
Hello. Are you coming around?

214
00:21:01,386 --> 00:21:02,220
Chris.

215
00:21:08,142 --> 00:21:10,645
It wasn't for long,
but he was fully unconscious,

216
00:21:11,229 --> 00:21:13,982
and his sense of balance isn't back yet.

217
00:21:14,065 --> 00:21:16,776
I suspect his sense of smell
and taste is impaired.

218
00:21:16,859 --> 00:21:17,694
Then…

219
00:21:17,777 --> 00:21:20,947
I can't say anything for sure
until a thorough checkup is done.

220
00:21:21,948 --> 00:21:25,785
There's a chance he might experience
a seizure or paralysis.

221
00:21:26,411 --> 00:21:28,496
You should take him to a general hospital.

222
00:21:29,038 --> 00:21:32,291
It's too dangerous
to let him compete further.

223
00:21:34,544 --> 00:21:36,504
I'll call for an ambulance.

224
00:21:38,881 --> 00:21:39,716
No.

225
00:21:42,719 --> 00:21:44,178
I won't give up the competition.

226
00:21:45,304 --> 00:21:46,180
Chris.

227
00:21:56,107 --> 00:21:59,193
All the dishes we prepared
will be served on schedule.

228
00:22:02,739 --> 00:22:03,656
Don't worry.

229
00:22:04,490 --> 00:22:06,034
I won't let you down.

230
00:22:06,117 --> 00:22:08,870
I'm sorry, but your will alone won't help.

231
00:22:08,953 --> 00:22:09,787
She's right.

232
00:22:09,871 --> 00:22:11,789
You need to rest.

233
00:22:12,373 --> 00:22:15,334
-A few more tests--
-I won't be cooking.

234
00:22:16,085 --> 00:22:17,754
Famiglia's dish that we'll serve today…

235
00:22:19,839 --> 00:22:20,673
will be prepared

236
00:22:21,549 --> 00:22:23,051
by Chef Choi Song-yi.

237
00:24:10,825 --> 00:24:12,076
Now that I'm back,

238
00:24:12,160 --> 00:24:14,954
I can't believe 40 years have passed.

239
00:24:15,621 --> 00:24:16,622
Back in my day,

240
00:24:16,706 --> 00:24:19,208
I had to share a desk with a friend.

241
00:24:19,292 --> 00:24:22,003
We drew a line down the middle
and told each other not to cross it,

242
00:24:22,086 --> 00:24:23,921
using pretty harsh language too.

243
00:24:25,756 --> 00:24:28,050
If someone brought
something nice for lunch,

244
00:24:28,134 --> 00:24:30,052
we got into fights trying to steal some.

245
00:24:31,053 --> 00:24:34,724
When you're that age,
you fight over the smallest things.

246
00:24:35,224 --> 00:24:38,060
Memories of those times
must be coming back.

247
00:24:38,144 --> 00:24:38,978
Yes.

248
00:24:41,439 --> 00:24:43,649
Keep going. Come on.

249
00:24:43,733 --> 00:24:45,359
What more am I supposed to do?

250
00:24:53,367 --> 00:24:55,369
Next, I'll interview
Chairwoman Moon Hae-seon

251
00:24:55,453 --> 00:24:56,954
of Pyeongyeon Group.

252
00:24:58,539 --> 00:24:59,540
Hello, ma'am.

253
00:25:00,833 --> 00:25:04,712
I was surprised to find out
you attended Haeon Elementary School.

254
00:25:04,795 --> 00:25:08,132
Is there a chef
you're particularly rooting for tonight?

255
00:25:12,011 --> 00:25:16,140
For this mission, the chefs must reinvent
street food everyone loved as children.

256
00:25:16,224 --> 00:25:20,228
Is there a dish you'd like to try today?

257
00:25:24,607 --> 00:25:28,569
What food is your personal favorite,
or is there anything you'd recommend?

258
00:25:30,613 --> 00:25:33,908
It doesn't have to be anything special.
For example…

259
00:25:34,617 --> 00:25:36,410
Something you ate as a child.

260
00:25:37,578 --> 00:25:40,665
Is there anything
you have fond memories of?

261
00:25:47,255 --> 00:25:48,089
Ma'am.

262
00:25:50,800 --> 00:25:51,884
Ma'am?

263
00:25:54,804 --> 00:25:55,721
Ma'am.

264
00:26:12,571 --> 00:26:14,573
I feel like dancing. I'm so excited.

265
00:26:17,785 --> 00:26:20,371
Let her try whatever she wants.

266
00:26:21,247 --> 00:26:22,707
There's no escape for her now.

267
00:26:29,797 --> 00:26:31,799
The way her lips trembled…

268
00:26:32,341 --> 00:26:34,218
Oh, Hyeong-koo. You missed out big time.

269
00:26:35,469 --> 00:26:36,470
My goodness.

270
00:26:37,346 --> 00:26:40,850
Who'd have thought that steely woman
would crumble like that?

271
00:26:40,933 --> 00:26:45,187
They do say that children
will be the death of you.

272
00:26:46,897 --> 00:26:49,233
We'll start getting calls
once the show ends.

273
00:26:49,817 --> 00:26:52,194
Now that they know
he's the chairwoman's son,

274
00:26:52,987 --> 00:26:55,614
reporters will swarm us in the morning.

275
00:26:57,908 --> 00:26:58,743
You.

276
00:26:59,285 --> 00:27:01,746
Don't you dare make
any official statements.

277
00:27:02,330 --> 00:27:04,665
Only say we're trying to
confirm the facts.

278
00:27:04,749 --> 00:27:06,208
Yes, of course.

279
00:27:07,001 --> 00:27:08,044
-Hyeong-koo.
-Yes?

280
00:27:09,003 --> 00:27:10,671
There's an order to things like this.

281
00:27:11,922 --> 00:27:12,965
-Look.
-Yes.

282
00:27:13,466 --> 00:27:15,176
Contact the experts

283
00:27:15,259 --> 00:27:17,720
and get them to post on social media.

284
00:27:18,554 --> 00:27:19,388
Spread the word.

285
00:27:19,472 --> 00:27:20,765
-"Spread the word."
-And then,

286
00:27:21,432 --> 00:27:24,602
contact a few U-Tube channels
that post videos on gossip and rumors.

287
00:27:25,728 --> 00:27:27,146
-Spill the tea.
-"Spill the tea"?

288
00:27:27,229 --> 00:27:30,983
Reporters these days rely on them
for stuff to write about.

289
00:27:32,651 --> 00:27:34,779
Sauce. That's it. Like pork cutlet sauce.

290
00:27:34,862 --> 00:27:37,656
Release it little by little,
not all at once.

291
00:27:37,740 --> 00:27:39,742
Drop by drop, okay?

292
00:27:42,328 --> 00:27:43,412
You get the idea?

293
00:27:43,496 --> 00:27:45,498
Just enough to keep everyone hooked.

294
00:27:46,999 --> 00:27:47,875
With structure.

295
00:27:47,958 --> 00:27:49,502
Yes, give it to them gradually.

296
00:27:49,585 --> 00:27:50,961
You know what I mean!

297
00:27:51,045 --> 00:27:53,589
Good dramas end episodes on a cliffhanger

298
00:27:53,672 --> 00:27:55,633
so the viewer can't wait for the next one.

299
00:27:55,716 --> 00:27:57,718
-You know what I mean, right?
-Yes.

300
00:27:57,802 --> 00:27:58,803
-Okay?
-Okay.

301
00:27:58,886 --> 00:28:00,346
I get what you're saying.

302
00:28:06,977 --> 00:28:08,521
Tasty memories?

303
00:28:08,604 --> 00:28:09,939
"Tasty memories"?

304
00:28:10,022 --> 00:28:12,233
I think that answer
best represents the mission…

305
00:28:12,316 --> 00:28:13,317
Producer Yoon.

306
00:28:13,401 --> 00:28:16,821
Too many viewers are saying
the interviews are getting boring.

307
00:28:17,321 --> 00:28:18,864
They're threatening to change the channel.

308
00:28:19,698 --> 00:28:20,866
That's one more interview done.

309
00:28:20,950 --> 00:28:22,118
We can't keep going.

310
00:28:22,201 --> 00:28:24,161
Let's show the teams in the kitchen.

311
00:28:26,622 --> 00:28:28,833
Can I do that without Ms. Seon's approval?

312
00:28:31,377 --> 00:28:33,546
-Let's cut to the audience for now.
-Okay.

313
00:28:33,629 --> 00:28:35,631
Get a camera on the main judges.

314
00:28:36,632 --> 00:28:37,633
Producer Kim.

315
00:28:38,259 --> 00:28:39,468
I can't do it.

316
00:28:39,552 --> 00:28:40,845
I can't do it, Chris.

317
00:28:40,928 --> 00:28:43,305
You can do it. You must do it.

318
00:28:43,389 --> 00:28:45,307
Chris. This is nonsense.

319
00:28:45,391 --> 00:28:47,393
How can I cook that important dish?

320
00:28:47,476 --> 00:28:48,477
You can.

321
00:28:49,103 --> 00:28:50,271
Do as we practiced this morning--

322
00:28:50,354 --> 00:28:51,605
I can't do it!

323
00:28:51,689 --> 00:28:52,940
I can't, Chris.

324
00:28:53,023 --> 00:28:54,942
-Song.
-Chris, please…

325
00:29:06,537 --> 00:29:07,746
Look at me.

326
00:29:08,372 --> 00:29:09,457
Look into my eyes.

327
00:29:10,708 --> 00:29:11,542
Song.

328
00:29:18,424 --> 00:29:19,758
It's all my fault.

329
00:29:20,885 --> 00:29:22,928
It's because of that stupid curse…

330
00:29:23,012 --> 00:29:25,347
I should've been more careful.

331
00:29:25,431 --> 00:29:26,891
I should've taken more precautions.

332
00:29:26,974 --> 00:29:29,727
Then you wouldn't have gotten hurt.

333
00:29:30,352 --> 00:29:32,229
It's all on me.

334
00:29:32,313 --> 00:29:35,065
It's all because of me and my darn curse.

335
00:29:39,028 --> 00:29:40,863
Why did you pick me?

336
00:29:40,946 --> 00:29:42,990
You should've chosen
someone with experience.

337
00:29:43,073 --> 00:29:45,951
Why pick a novice like me
with zero experience?

338
00:29:46,577 --> 00:29:48,913
Look at me. I can't do anything.

339
00:29:49,663 --> 00:29:52,416
Even in this terrible situation,
all I can do is cry.

340
00:29:52,500 --> 00:29:54,543
Why did you pick me?

341
00:29:55,127 --> 00:29:58,047
Why pick useless, incompetent me? Why?

342
00:29:58,130 --> 00:29:59,715
Why?

343
00:30:04,678 --> 00:30:05,888
Because the owner of Famiglia…

344
00:30:07,765 --> 00:30:08,849
is you.

345
00:30:24,740 --> 00:30:25,783
The owner of Famiglia

346
00:30:27,159 --> 00:30:29,411
isn't me or anyone else.

347
00:30:30,079 --> 00:30:31,330
It's you, Choi Song-yi.

348
00:30:33,457 --> 00:30:34,959
Once the competition's over and I leave,

349
00:30:36,877 --> 00:30:38,128
who will manage Famiglia?

350
00:30:40,214 --> 00:30:41,090
Your aunt?

351
00:30:41,715 --> 00:30:42,633
Chef Park?

352
00:30:43,884 --> 00:30:44,718
No.

353
00:30:47,137 --> 00:30:48,597
The one to lead Famiglia

354
00:30:50,516 --> 00:30:51,892
is you, Choi Song-yi.

355
00:30:56,605 --> 00:30:57,523
Have you forgotten?

356
00:31:00,401 --> 00:31:01,569
Your father.

357
00:31:04,738 --> 00:31:06,198
Pyeongyeon killed your father.

358
00:31:08,242 --> 00:31:09,493
We must beat them.

359
00:31:11,078 --> 00:31:12,162
Together.

360
00:31:16,500 --> 00:31:19,336
This dish… No, this competition.

361
00:31:23,591 --> 00:31:24,967
It all began

362
00:31:26,677 --> 00:31:28,846
when you smelled the lotus flower
and found Jehyangju.

363
00:31:30,681 --> 00:31:33,434
We didn't stand a chance
if it hadn't been for you.

364
00:31:37,479 --> 00:31:38,397
Song.

365
00:31:39,064 --> 00:31:39,940
You have something

366
00:31:40,774 --> 00:31:42,109
that no one else has.

367
00:31:43,360 --> 00:31:45,696
A great sense and instinct. Trust me.

368
00:31:46,655 --> 00:31:47,615
You will manage…

369
00:31:50,200 --> 00:31:51,493
better than anyone.

370
00:31:59,418 --> 00:32:00,544
Remember this.

371
00:32:00,628 --> 00:32:03,631
Cooking is less about skill and technique…

372
00:32:05,716 --> 00:32:07,468
than it is about memory and imagination.

373
00:32:11,305 --> 00:32:12,431
Song. Listen.

374
00:32:17,436 --> 00:32:19,480
Remember the pitch and volume
of this sound.

375
00:32:20,773 --> 00:32:22,274
Don't rely only on your sight.

376
00:32:22,358 --> 00:32:24,318
Sometimes, sound is more informative.

377
00:32:24,401 --> 00:32:26,195
And don't trust the thermometer too much.

378
00:32:26,278 --> 00:32:28,530
For this dish, that can be a trap.

379
00:32:30,324 --> 00:32:31,200
Okay.

380
00:32:32,993 --> 00:32:33,827
Song.

381
00:32:34,620 --> 00:32:37,748
Your nose and tongue can distract you.

382
00:32:38,582 --> 00:32:40,584
What's more important
than focusing on the senses

383
00:32:41,543 --> 00:32:43,253
is what is inside the chef's head.

384
00:32:44,213 --> 00:32:45,130
The big picture.

385
00:32:45,798 --> 00:32:47,132
It's not just about the taste.

386
00:32:47,216 --> 00:32:49,176
Think about the idea you want to express.

387
00:32:49,259 --> 00:32:50,511
That's the point.

388
00:32:55,140 --> 00:32:56,850
Remember just two things in this round.

389
00:32:57,685 --> 00:32:59,478
Memory and imagination.

390
00:33:19,998 --> 00:33:21,625
ONGOJISIM

391
00:33:34,304 --> 00:33:37,683
It looks like
you're cleaning a sea anemone.

392
00:33:38,517 --> 00:33:40,352
Yes. It's a swimming anemone.

393
00:33:40,436 --> 00:33:44,815
It's a unique species
that's only found in deeper waters.

394
00:33:44,898 --> 00:33:46,400
Not only does it look different,

395
00:33:46,483 --> 00:33:49,278
but its flesh has more texture
than the ordinary anemone.

396
00:33:49,361 --> 00:33:51,155
It can be served boiled.

397
00:33:52,948 --> 00:33:56,952
I didn't know swimming anemones
could be found on the Haeon coast.

398
00:33:57,453 --> 00:34:00,831
May I ask what you plan to use it for?

399
00:34:02,499 --> 00:34:07,296
Oh, it looks like your sous-chef
is sticking noodles to a square frame.

400
00:34:08,172 --> 00:34:09,840
It reminds me of lasagna.

401
00:34:09,923 --> 00:34:11,592
I'm making a noodle plate.

402
00:34:11,675 --> 00:34:12,926
A noodle plate?

403
00:34:13,010 --> 00:34:15,512
I'm making a plate with actual noodles.

404
00:34:15,596 --> 00:34:17,973
Oh, a plate made with noodles.

405
00:34:18,932 --> 00:34:21,310
Wouldn't that leak?

406
00:34:21,393 --> 00:34:22,895
It might be hard to eat from.

407
00:34:23,520 --> 00:34:24,354
It would, wouldn't it?

408
00:34:25,606 --> 00:34:28,233
Naturally, we thought of a way
to prevent that.

409
00:34:50,422 --> 00:34:54,343
IT CAN'T HAVE BEEN EASY TO GIVE A TWIST
TO A FAMILIAR FOOD LIKE GIMBAP

410
00:34:54,426 --> 00:34:59,306
We thought gimbap was a Korean street food
we couldn't leave out.

411
00:34:59,389 --> 00:35:01,558
We wanted to turn
that common, ordinary food

412
00:35:01,642 --> 00:35:04,770
into something glamorous and unusual.

413
00:35:04,853 --> 00:35:08,482
For tradition to continue,
it must be accompanied by innovation.

414
00:35:09,191 --> 00:35:11,652
So we got the idea
to switch the insides and outsides.

415
00:35:12,236 --> 00:35:15,739
When I was in school,
the cafeteria used to serve celery.

416
00:35:16,281 --> 00:35:18,784
The kids hated it,

417
00:35:18,867 --> 00:35:21,245
so the cooks coated it
with a thick layer of peanut butter.

418
00:35:22,246 --> 00:35:25,374
The nutty peanut butter
tricked the kids into eating it.

419
00:35:25,457 --> 00:35:28,460
Of course, some kids wiped off
the peanut butter with their fingers

420
00:35:28,544 --> 00:35:30,671
and threw out the celery sticks.

421
00:35:31,380 --> 00:35:32,589
That reminded us,

422
00:35:33,382 --> 00:35:37,678
another nutritious vegetable
that kids dislike is carrots.

423
00:35:37,761 --> 00:35:39,596
That's a core ingredient of most gimbap.

424
00:35:40,222 --> 00:35:44,601
We decided to wrap the rice and stuffing
with a thin, blanched layer of carrot.

425
00:35:45,185 --> 00:35:48,272
Then we'll wrap the outside
with a strip of dried seaweed.

426
00:35:49,106 --> 00:35:53,318
It's a special dried seaweed
seasoned with savory hawthorn berry oil.

427
00:35:53,402 --> 00:35:56,154
Carrots usually go on the inside,
and dried seaweed on the outside.

428
00:35:56,238 --> 00:35:58,073
This time, they envelop each other.

429
00:35:58,156 --> 00:36:00,492
Our gimbap has no inside or outside.

430
00:36:01,118 --> 00:36:02,494
It's not the traditional recipe.

431
00:36:03,495 --> 00:36:04,621
We got the idea for the name

432
00:36:04,705 --> 00:36:09,668
from Belgian artist René Magritte's
This Is Not A Pipe.

433
00:36:12,880 --> 00:36:14,631
"This is not gimbap."

434
00:36:16,049 --> 00:36:18,468
THIS IS NOT GIMBAP

435
00:36:20,804 --> 00:36:22,514
No good.

436
00:36:23,515 --> 00:36:25,726
I don't smell the cheongju at all.

437
00:36:32,524 --> 00:36:33,358
You're right.

438
00:36:34,318 --> 00:36:38,572
We neutralized the spiciness
with sugar, white miso, and tomato sauce,

439
00:36:39,615 --> 00:36:41,950
but the gochujang
has too much body to begin with.

440
00:36:42,868 --> 00:36:46,246
It would work with a hard liquor,
but it won't go well with cheongju.

441
00:36:46,330 --> 00:36:47,456
Right.

442
00:36:47,539 --> 00:36:49,541
This is total madness.

443
00:36:50,042 --> 00:36:52,377
Should we have come up
with something else?

444
00:36:54,379 --> 00:36:55,213
Chef.

445
00:36:55,297 --> 00:36:56,673
Will you check this for me?

446
00:36:57,466 --> 00:36:59,676
It's too soft to use as sundae casing.

447
00:37:02,262 --> 00:37:03,722
What should we do?

448
00:37:04,264 --> 00:37:07,559
Should we scrap the crispy vegetables
with tteokbokki sauce

449
00:37:08,602 --> 00:37:09,436
and make something else?

450
00:37:12,064 --> 00:37:13,023
I don't know.

451
00:37:14,733 --> 00:37:17,235
Nothing else comes to mind.

452
00:37:17,819 --> 00:37:18,987
Shall we switch to soy sauce?

453
00:37:19,071 --> 00:37:21,823
Then we'd need to deep-fry meat
instead of vegetables,

454
00:37:21,907 --> 00:37:23,575
but that might be too boring.

455
00:37:26,286 --> 00:37:28,997
I should've believed him.

456
00:37:29,915 --> 00:37:32,334
I thought it was a trick.

457
00:37:32,417 --> 00:37:34,670
-Darn it.
-What?

458
00:37:35,253 --> 00:37:38,090
Who would've thought
it was actually cheongju?

459
00:37:38,757 --> 00:37:42,302
Never in my wildest dreams
did I think he'd give us

460
00:37:42,886 --> 00:37:45,055
such important information.

461
00:37:45,138 --> 00:37:45,973
What do you mean?

462
00:37:47,391 --> 00:37:48,809
Huh? What?

463
00:37:48,892 --> 00:37:52,020
What you just said.
Who gave you what information?

464
00:37:54,064 --> 00:37:56,233
Well, you see…

465
00:37:56,316 --> 00:37:58,151
Did someone give you
a heads-up about cheongju?

466
00:37:58,777 --> 00:37:59,611
Well?

467
00:38:00,612 --> 00:38:01,488
Did they?

468
00:38:07,035 --> 00:38:08,578
Actually,

469
00:38:08,662 --> 00:38:10,914
this morning in the training center lobby,

470
00:38:10,998 --> 00:38:13,583
just when Joshua and I were passing by,

471
00:38:14,126 --> 00:38:15,961
Chris called out to us.

472
00:38:16,586 --> 00:38:19,256
He said he thought
our mission would involve cheongju.

473
00:38:20,632 --> 00:38:23,969
He explained
what his teammate had figured out.

474
00:38:24,052 --> 00:38:25,804
The 100-day maturation period

475
00:38:25,887 --> 00:38:27,514
and something about the Liquor Tax Act

476
00:38:27,597 --> 00:38:29,975
forcing us to call it yakju
and not cheongju.

477
00:38:30,058 --> 00:38:31,560
He said stuff like that.

478
00:38:32,102 --> 00:38:32,936
And?

479
00:38:33,687 --> 00:38:35,981
Why didn't you tell us any of that?

480
00:38:36,064 --> 00:38:37,649
I obviously thought

481
00:38:38,734 --> 00:38:41,403
Chris had made it up to mess with us.

482
00:38:45,323 --> 00:38:47,993
Guys. Think about it.

483
00:38:48,076 --> 00:38:49,953
It's a cutthroat competition.

484
00:38:50,037 --> 00:38:53,707
Who would share
their hard-earned information for free?

485
00:38:53,790 --> 00:38:56,501
If they did,
it would be no other than to trick us.

486
00:38:56,585 --> 00:38:57,627
Sure.

487
00:38:59,463 --> 00:39:01,965
Don't blame me too much.

488
00:39:02,632 --> 00:39:04,342
The regret is killing me right now.

489
00:39:05,469 --> 00:39:08,430
How was I to know the mission
would actually be about cheongju?

490
00:39:09,014 --> 00:39:10,348
Is that all you can say?

491
00:39:11,224 --> 00:39:14,644
Chris shared the info to help us,
and you ignored that help.

492
00:39:14,728 --> 00:39:17,564
All three teams agreed
to share what we found out.

493
00:39:17,647 --> 00:39:21,193
That's why Chris K. decided
to tell us what he knew.

494
00:39:21,818 --> 00:39:23,487
Were you lied to your whole life?

495
00:39:25,906 --> 00:39:27,491
Why are you so pessimistic?

496
00:39:29,326 --> 00:39:30,160
What?

497
00:39:30,243 --> 00:39:33,080
If something like that had gone down,

498
00:39:33,163 --> 00:39:36,208
you should've at least told me about it.

499
00:39:36,291 --> 00:39:38,627
That's the least you can do
for your teammate.

500
00:39:38,710 --> 00:39:40,504
Mr. Yang, don't you have manners?

501
00:39:41,129 --> 00:39:42,798
Watch it, Park-san.

502
00:39:43,590 --> 00:39:45,383
I don't like your tone.

503
00:39:45,926 --> 00:39:47,594
Are you suggesting I messed up on purpose?

504
00:39:47,677 --> 00:39:49,888
I told you not to call me that, didn't I?

505
00:39:50,555 --> 00:39:54,142
I'm not Japanese.
I'm Korean, you got that?

506
00:40:00,524 --> 00:40:02,317
You may see this competition

507
00:40:02,859 --> 00:40:05,028
as some sort of game
where you cook for fun.

508
00:40:05,112 --> 00:40:06,321
It's not like that for me.

509
00:40:06,404 --> 00:40:08,073
I promised my daughter.

510
00:40:08,156 --> 00:40:10,700
She could pass away any day,
and it's for her sake

511
00:40:10,784 --> 00:40:13,787
that I risked my reputation
to enter this competition.

512
00:40:15,705 --> 00:40:16,665
Mr. Yang.

513
00:40:17,874 --> 00:40:19,126
Let's take this seriously.

514
00:40:19,209 --> 00:40:21,419
Did you ever give it your all
since you entered?

515
00:40:21,503 --> 00:40:24,005
You slack off and show off all the time.

516
00:40:24,089 --> 00:40:26,216
Aren't you embarrassed?
What would your sous-chef think?

517
00:40:26,842 --> 00:40:27,676
Hey.

518
00:40:28,635 --> 00:40:31,263
Park Je-hyeon, you punk.

519
00:40:31,346 --> 00:40:32,264
What?

520
00:40:32,347 --> 00:40:34,474
Oh, you are ashamed of what he'd think?

521
00:40:34,558 --> 00:40:36,143
I guess even you care about saving face.

522
00:40:36,226 --> 00:40:37,936
Shut your mouth right now,

523
00:40:38,603 --> 00:40:40,438
or I'll slit it ear to ear.

524
00:40:40,522 --> 00:40:41,857
You're so vulgar.

525
00:40:41,940 --> 00:40:43,859
You're crude to the very end.

526
00:40:44,359 --> 00:40:46,027
What a lowlife.

527
00:40:46,820 --> 00:40:48,029
Why you…

528
00:40:49,197 --> 00:40:51,241
-What?
-Think you're the only serious one?

529
00:40:51,324 --> 00:40:52,450
You care about your daughter?

530
00:40:52,534 --> 00:40:53,785
-You worry about her.
-Chef.

531
00:40:53,869 --> 00:40:55,787
If I don't win this competition,

532
00:40:55,871 --> 00:40:58,415
I might get butchered!

533
00:40:58,498 --> 00:41:01,459
I'm as serious about this as you are!

534
00:41:01,543 --> 00:41:03,503
-Darn you!
-Please, Chef!

535
00:41:07,090 --> 00:41:08,383
-Let go.
-Chef!

536
00:41:08,466 --> 00:41:10,135
-Let go of me.
-Chef, please.

537
00:41:15,348 --> 00:41:16,349
Please!

538
00:41:17,434 --> 00:41:18,351
Let go.

539
00:41:18,935 --> 00:41:21,188
Chef, the sign is back on!

540
00:41:50,175 --> 00:41:54,095
THE JUDGE NOTICED
THE ICY TENSION BETWEEN YOU

541
00:41:54,179 --> 00:41:56,306
Oh, come on.

542
00:41:56,389 --> 00:41:58,266
It wasn't like that at all.

543
00:41:58,350 --> 00:41:59,893
Everything was just fine.

544
00:41:59,976 --> 00:42:04,981
You have no idea how good our teamwork is.

545
00:42:05,857 --> 00:42:09,319
When two chefs get into
a heated discussion about food,

546
00:42:09,402 --> 00:42:11,488
voices can get raised.

547
00:42:11,571 --> 00:42:14,950
I see the judge misunderstood
what was happening.

548
00:42:16,326 --> 00:42:19,579
Are the walls paper thin?

549
00:42:24,042 --> 00:42:25,502
The walls are concrete?

550
00:42:27,545 --> 00:42:30,924
I see. They're made of concrete…

551
00:42:31,925 --> 00:42:36,596
Do support our team, Dragon-Sak.

552
00:42:36,680 --> 00:42:39,891
We're good!

553
00:42:41,685 --> 00:42:46,731
WE HEARD YOU EXPERIENCED DIFFICULTY
PAIRING CHEONGJU WITH TTEOKBOKKI SAUCE

554
00:42:47,232 --> 00:42:48,817
Yes. That's true.

555
00:42:50,026 --> 00:42:52,779
The gochujang's heat
and the cheongju's light flavor

556
00:42:52,862 --> 00:42:55,240
were hard to bring together.

557
00:42:55,323 --> 00:43:00,287
So I suggested adding an outer layer,

558
00:43:00,370 --> 00:43:01,705
like the sugar coating on a pill.

559
00:43:02,205 --> 00:43:04,833
We made egg-shaped rice cakes

560
00:43:04,916 --> 00:43:07,836
filled with frozen tteokbokki sauce,

561
00:43:07,919 --> 00:43:10,213
jjajang sauce, or curry sauce.

562
00:43:10,297 --> 00:43:11,798
The final step is

563
00:43:11,881 --> 00:43:15,218
to coat them in semolina and fry them,

564
00:43:15,302 --> 00:43:19,264
giving them a nice crust like an eggshell.

565
00:43:23,476 --> 00:43:26,479
You can peel it
like a boiled egg as you eat.

566
00:43:26,563 --> 00:43:29,024
We wanted to make the food fun.

567
00:44:12,567 --> 00:44:13,401
Ms. Seon.

568
00:44:14,486 --> 00:44:16,488
Ms. Choi Song-yi is back in Lab Two.

569
00:44:20,241 --> 00:44:22,494
It looks like
she'll do something on her own.

570
00:44:41,971 --> 00:44:43,848
We'll put Lab Two on air shortly.

571
00:44:44,474 --> 00:44:45,934
Prepare for the cut.

572
00:45:28,768 --> 00:45:29,602
Hey.

573
00:45:32,063 --> 00:45:33,481
What are you doing?

574
00:45:36,776 --> 00:45:38,236
I asked what you're doing.

575
00:45:39,154 --> 00:45:40,196
I'm making the second dish

576
00:45:41,281 --> 00:45:42,657
that incorporates the cheonghyang.

577
00:45:43,700 --> 00:45:45,201
What on earth do you think…

578
00:45:46,035 --> 00:45:47,162
I'll make it.

579
00:45:48,121 --> 00:45:49,497
Our team's second dish.

580
00:45:51,666 --> 00:45:54,586
Where is Chef Chris right now?
When will he be back?

581
00:46:18,359 --> 00:46:19,360
The knife.

582
00:46:21,654 --> 00:46:22,572
Put it down.

583
00:46:28,828 --> 00:46:30,538
You can put it down now…

584
00:46:32,665 --> 00:46:33,666
Ms. Choi.

585
00:46:45,011 --> 00:46:45,970
Don't worry.

586
00:46:48,389 --> 00:46:49,682
As for the second dish,

587
00:46:50,725 --> 00:46:52,352
we'll replace it with something else.

588
00:46:53,102 --> 00:46:54,020
I need you

589
00:46:55,730 --> 00:46:56,648
to help me instead,

590
00:46:57,690 --> 00:46:58,525
Ms. Choi.

591
00:47:14,749 --> 00:47:15,583
Remember this.

592
00:47:17,043 --> 00:47:19,546
Cooking is less about skill and technique…

593
00:47:21,548 --> 00:47:23,174
than it is about memory and imagination.

594
00:47:29,138 --> 00:47:30,390
The owner of Famiglia.

595
00:47:31,057 --> 00:47:32,350
It's you, Choi Song-yi.

596
00:47:34,060 --> 00:47:35,645
The one to lead Famiglia

597
00:47:36,187 --> 00:47:37,689
is you, Choi Song-yi.

598
00:47:39,816 --> 00:47:40,984
Your father.

599
00:47:42,735 --> 00:47:44,070
Pyeongyeon killed your father.

600
00:47:44,862 --> 00:47:46,573
We must beat them.

601
00:47:47,699 --> 00:47:48,700
Together.

602
00:47:49,659 --> 00:47:51,202
Song. You have something

603
00:47:51,828 --> 00:47:53,413
that no one else has.

604
00:47:54,664 --> 00:47:57,000
A great sense and instinct. Trust me.

605
00:47:58,001 --> 00:48:00,753
You will manage better than anyone.

606
00:48:29,616 --> 00:48:30,491
Come on.

607
00:48:37,457 --> 00:48:38,374
Ms. Choi.

608
00:48:39,917 --> 00:48:42,629
I'll mince and mix
the poultry and wildfowl meat.

609
00:48:42,712 --> 00:48:45,173
The chicken, pheasant,
and quail, to be exact.

610
00:48:45,256 --> 00:48:47,342
I'll wrap the meat in lotus leaves,
smoke it,

611
00:48:47,425 --> 00:48:49,344
cut it into blocks, and fry them.

612
00:48:49,427 --> 00:48:51,304
I'll use dried lotus roots for smoke.

613
00:48:52,472 --> 00:48:55,350
I just soaked
maple tree chips in Jehyangju.

614
00:48:55,433 --> 00:48:58,728
Once they absorb the liquor's aroma,
I'll dry them for use.

615
00:48:58,811 --> 00:49:00,355
I'll use a smoke gun.

616
00:49:00,438 --> 00:49:03,524
The whole point of this dish
is the memories it evokes.

617
00:49:03,608 --> 00:49:07,570
I'll focus on the sense of smell,
the most basic human sense of all.

618
00:49:08,196 --> 00:49:09,030
The cheonghyang.

619
00:49:13,701 --> 00:49:14,535
What's that?

620
00:49:14,619 --> 00:49:17,080
It's water from Mount Gyesul,
the main ingredient of Jehyangju.

621
00:49:17,163 --> 00:49:21,084
We added wildflowers from the mountain
and left it in the fridge overnight.

622
00:49:21,167 --> 00:49:24,754
I'll use the water and the flowers inside.

623
00:49:39,102 --> 00:49:40,353
Let's get things ready.

624
00:49:40,436 --> 00:49:41,312
Yes, Chef.

625
00:50:36,534 --> 00:50:37,368
Dad.

626
00:50:39,620 --> 00:50:40,455
Dad.

627
00:50:42,039 --> 00:50:43,332
What are you thinking about?

628
00:50:53,426 --> 00:50:54,844
I'm thinking about your mom.

629
00:50:59,724 --> 00:51:00,558
My mom?

630
00:51:01,684 --> 00:51:02,518
Yes.

631
00:51:03,978 --> 00:51:05,438
Do those flowers…

632
00:51:08,900 --> 00:51:10,109
remind you of Mom?

633
00:51:20,828 --> 00:51:22,163
They're beautiful.

634
00:51:26,876 --> 00:51:27,877
They're beautiful…

635
00:51:30,338 --> 00:51:31,214
and sad.

636
00:51:39,138 --> 00:51:40,389
Just like your mom.

637
00:52:59,677 --> 00:53:00,511
What happened?

638
00:53:00,595 --> 00:53:01,596
What is it, Han?

639
00:53:03,973 --> 00:53:05,266
Oh dear…

640
00:53:07,310 --> 00:53:08,811
Don't just stand there.

641
00:53:08,895 --> 00:53:10,187
Let's put it in another container.

642
00:53:23,868 --> 00:53:25,119
Can't we just throw it out?

643
00:53:26,287 --> 00:53:27,246
It smells rotten.

644
00:53:27,872 --> 00:53:28,956
Why throw this out?

645
00:53:29,040 --> 00:53:30,124
It's fine.

646
00:53:30,207 --> 00:53:33,294
We'll throw out the bottom half,
but we can save what's on top.

647
00:53:34,253 --> 00:53:35,504
This is precious stuff.

648
00:53:37,006 --> 00:53:41,135
I begged our landlord to let me bury this
in the garden for three months.

649
00:53:41,218 --> 00:53:44,639
If we lose this,
we might have to travel all the way to LA.

650
00:53:48,017 --> 00:53:50,102
There is no Koreatown here,

651
00:53:50,186 --> 00:53:51,854
so it's harder to find Korean ingredients.

652
00:53:51,938 --> 00:53:53,272
Be more careful from now on, okay?

653
00:53:59,904 --> 00:54:01,322
Exactly.

654
00:54:02,156 --> 00:54:03,532
Can't we stop moving around?

655
00:54:06,827 --> 00:54:08,412
Can't we stay in one place, Dad?

656
00:54:27,598 --> 00:54:28,474
Han.

657
00:54:29,684 --> 00:54:30,768
It's tough, isn't it?

658
00:54:32,895 --> 00:54:34,021
Hang in there.

659
00:54:35,147 --> 00:54:36,107
Just a bit longer.

660
00:54:36,190 --> 00:54:37,608
For how long?

661
00:54:40,319 --> 00:54:42,446
How long do we have to be on the run for?

662
00:54:43,197 --> 00:54:45,700
How long do we have to
keep running like fugitives?

663
00:54:52,039 --> 00:54:53,499
Will Mom kill us if she finds us?

664
00:54:55,084 --> 00:54:56,502
Does she want us dead?

665
00:54:57,670 --> 00:55:00,047
-Han!
-Does she want to keep us apart so badly

666
00:55:00,131 --> 00:55:02,341
that she would actually kill us?

667
00:55:18,357 --> 00:55:19,275
I'm sorry, Han.

668
00:55:28,200 --> 00:55:29,243
Han.

669
00:55:38,711 --> 00:55:39,795
Han.

670
00:56:31,931 --> 00:56:33,849
WAIVER OF PARENTAL RIGHTS

671
00:56:36,102 --> 00:56:37,728
Dad!

672
00:56:38,687 --> 00:56:40,564
Dad!

673
00:56:59,959 --> 00:57:02,878
SCHOOL NURSE'S OFFICE

674
00:57:47,631 --> 00:57:48,465
What's this?

675
00:57:58,934 --> 00:57:59,935
What's going on?

676
00:58:05,107 --> 00:58:07,484
What on earth is happening now?

677
00:58:07,568 --> 00:58:09,486
What is going on?

678
00:58:09,570 --> 00:58:10,654
It's a power outage.

679
00:58:10,738 --> 00:58:11,822
What?

680
00:58:13,157 --> 00:58:14,116
A blackout?

681
00:58:27,296 --> 00:58:29,298
The west wing circuit overloaded.

682
00:58:29,381 --> 00:58:30,466
What do we do?

683
00:58:54,949 --> 00:58:56,533
It looks like a power outage.

684
00:59:14,677 --> 00:59:15,511
Han.

685
00:59:22,977 --> 00:59:25,437
Find the Jehyangju material
we had ready earlier.

686
00:59:25,521 --> 00:59:26,438
On it!

687
00:59:27,523 --> 00:59:29,275
Captain Cook, we're in trouble over here.

688
00:59:29,358 --> 00:59:31,568
I'll pull up some footage on Jehyangju.

689
00:59:31,652 --> 00:59:33,195
Stall for as long as you can.

690
01:00:36,508 --> 01:00:37,926
This time,

691
01:00:38,010 --> 01:00:39,762
we can't let it go her way.

692
01:00:40,721 --> 01:00:41,555
We have 27 minutes left.

693
01:00:42,056 --> 01:00:43,223
Can it be done?

694
01:00:43,891 --> 01:00:47,519
It's time to announce the final ranking.

695
01:00:48,604 --> 01:00:51,231
It was suffocating,
lying low all this time, wasn't it?

696
01:00:51,315 --> 01:00:53,359
-He's in there, right?
-Can we get a comment?

697
01:00:53,442 --> 01:00:54,610
What does this mean?

698
01:00:54,693 --> 01:00:55,944
Pyeongyeon Group's chairwoman's…

699
01:00:56,820 --> 01:00:58,447
Was that the reason?

700
01:01:00,366 --> 01:01:01,533
For my father…

701
01:01:03,077 --> 01:01:04,370
who died in torment.

702
01:01:09,208 --> 01:01:12,211
Subtitle: Jennifer Jean Lim
